  │ [38;5;153mauth/middleware.js[39m │ [38;5;153mrequireAuth[39m → 401 or [38;5;153mreq.user[39m                 │ token.js         │
  ├────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mauth/routes.js[39m     │ the 5 endpoints                               │ all of the above │
  └────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┘

  [1mEndpoints:[0m [38;5;153mPOST /auth/register[39m, [38;5;153mPOST /auth/login[39m, [38;5;153mPOST /auth/forgot-password[39m, [38;5;153mPOST /auth/reset-password[39m, and [38;5;153mGET [39m
  [38;5;153m/auth/me[39m as the protected-route proof.

  [1mTwo security decisions worth naming[0m, since you can't approve what you can't see:

  1. [38;5;153m/auth/forgot-password[39m returns the same [38;5;153m202[39m whether or not the email exists — no account enumeration. The link only
     appears on stdout.
  2. Reset tokens are single-use, 15-minute expiry, stored hashed, and [1mall of a user's JWTs stay valid after a password
[0m     [1mreset[0m. Proper revocation needs a token version or denylist; that's real work and out of scope for a POC. I'm
     flagging it as a known gap, not hiding it.

  [1mTesting:[0m [38;5;153mnode:test[39m (built in, no dependency). [38;5;153mapp.js[39m splits into [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (exports the app) and [38;5;153mserver.js[39m (listens), so
  tests can drive it without a live port.

  [1mError handling:[0m 400 on malformed input, 401 on bad credentials or bad token, 409 on duplicate email. No stack traces
  to clients.

  ---

  Two notes on what I'm [3mnot[0m doing, so it's explicit rather than discovered later: there's no rate limiting on login or
  reset, and no refresh-token flow. Both are the right call for a POC and both are wrong for production.

  Now writing this to a spec, then turning it into an implementation plan.

